WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.950
What do you know about the American Civil
War?

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.710
Just saying these words — “the Civil War”
— probably brings some familiar images to

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:12.780
mind.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.820
Northern soldiers in Union blue marching against
Southern troops in Confederate gray.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.789
Epic battles at Shiloh and Gettysburg.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:24.369
Abraham Lincoln’s Emancipation Proclamation
and the end of slavery.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:27.369
The victory of the Union over the Confederacy.

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:30.880
But there’s something else you should know
about that era — something that ties these

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.020
events together, and that you probably didn’t
learn in school.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.260
The Civil War was also America’s greatest
left-wing revolution.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.800
Here’s how.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.230
In the early nineteenth century, African slavery
was the foundation of social and economic

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.440
life in the American South.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.310
Millions of black workers were owned as property
by wealthy plantation owners, who denied them

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:55.510
education, controlled their family lives,
and reaped the fruits of their labor.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.600
This system was wildly profitable, and the
wealth it produced allowed a tiny elite to

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.180
dominate Southern and national politics.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.970
And before the Civil War, it was expanding
across the Western Hemisphere.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:12.120
In 1860, between the United States, Brazil
and Cuba, there were more enslaved people

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.710
than ever before.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.860
For years, a small group of American abolitionists
denounced slavery as an abomination.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.750
These radical activists were scorned by much
of society, and ignored by both of the two

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.680
mainstream political parties.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.600
But in the 1850s, as slaveholders tried to
extend slavery across the West, their opponents

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.820
made a breakthrough.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.180
The old two-party system melted down, and
the new Republican Party won massive Northern

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:40.330
support by opposing slavery’s expansion,
while putting bondage itself on a road to

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:41.549
“ultimate extinction.”

00:01:41.549 --> 00:01:46.280
The abolitionists broke through, in part,
because they named “the Slave Power” as

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.790
the oppressive ruling class it was.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:53.240
They pointed out that slaveholders were just
1 percent of the population, but controlled

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.430
every branch of government.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:59.460
They argued that black bondage was not just
morally wrong, but a threat to free labor

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:00.460
everywhere.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:05.390
The struggle between antislavery and slavery,
said Abraham Lincoln, was part of “the eternal

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.259
struggle” between “the common right of
humanity” and “the divine right of kings,”

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:13.920
“the same spirit that says, ‘you toil
and work and earn bread, and I’ll eat it.’”

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.880
It was the common worker, black or white,
against the rich.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.190
These populist assaults on the ruling class
helped Lincoln’s party win the election

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:23.720
of 1860.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.730
Contemporaries called it a “political revolution.”

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:31.060
Its threat to slaveholders was so serious
that they formed their own separate republic,

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.010
the Confederate States of America.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.670
In the Civil War that followed, the stakes
were clear.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:40.200
The Union fought against human bondage, for
a democratic majority, and for the dignity

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.530
of all labor.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.700
The Confederacy fought to preserve bondage
and elite rule.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.470
Confederates fought, as Lincoln told Congress
in 1862, to put “capital” — wealth,

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:55.290
measured in human beings — “above labor
in the structure of government.”

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:59.239
Enslaved laborers understood these stakes
— hundreds of thousands fled to the Union

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:02.800
lines even before emancipation was proclaimed.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.520
Radical abolitionists knew it too: Frederick
Douglass barnstormed the country, rousing

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.750
black men to enlist in the Union army.

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:14.390
By war’s end, around 200,000 black soldiers
and sailors had taken up arms to crush the

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:15.390
rebellion.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:18.560
The cause of the Union was the cause of the
international Left.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.550
While conservative aristocrats and capitalists
in Europe sympathized with the Confederacy,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.300
radicals and labor organizations supported
the Union.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.460
In 1864 a German journalist named Karl Marx
declared that “the American antislavery

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:35.670
war” would launch “a new era of ascendency”
for “the working classes.”

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:40.590
Emancipation, as Marx understood, was not
a middle-class reform project: it was a true

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.390
social revolution.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:48.220
Only in the United States and in Haiti was
emancipation sudden, uncompensated, and enforced

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.599
by former slaves themselves.

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:53.390
That revolution remains incomplete.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:58.780
After the war, former slaves and radical Republicans
were defeated by what the scholar W.E.B.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:04.230
Du Bois called “a counterrevolution of property,”
led by former slaveholders and conservative

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.390
businessmen.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:09.120
Under the terror of Jim Crow segregation,
many of the Civil War’s victories for labor

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.170
and democracy were rolled back.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.190
But not all.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:17.019
The destruction of American chattel slavery
was a historic victory.

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:21.680
It meant that black southerners were able
to create the schools and churches that seeded

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:23.550
the fight against Jim Crow.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:28.270
It radically expanded American democracy and
made the modern labor and civil rights movements

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:29.560
possible.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:33.660
The Civil War was America’s greatest left-wing
revolution.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.080
Even now, its work remains unfinished.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.360
But every left-wing struggle — for the rule
of democratic majorities, for the rights of

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:45.750
labor over property, and for meaningful freedom
and equality — can claim its legacy.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:05:01.990
I’m Matt Karp, associate professor of history
at Princeton University, for the Gravel Institute.

